    "gmfe_insample": 1.287,
    "n": 103,
    "r2_volume": 0.871,
    "volume_tau_exp": 0.6997,
    "volume_gear_exp": -0.1674,
    "volume_coef_m3": 4.17574e-05,
    "q_coef": 0.4011,
    "q_tau_exp": -0.0976,
    "q_tau_stderr": 0.0211,
    "q_gear_exp": 0.3157,
    "q_gear_stderr": 0.0252,
    "q_r2": 0.639,
    "q_gmfe_insample": 1.187,
    "note": "s > 0 says the effective shear stress RISES with machine size \u2014 a bigger motor cools better, carries more back-iron and runs more poles. k > 0 says the rotor's share of the package FALLS as the reduction grows, which is the gearbox crowding it out; it is why a harmonic module and a QDD cannot share one constant. Neither is a fudge: both are what is left over once r^2*L is imposed, and both come with a standard error. Absolute check: the package shear stress divided by the independently measured active fraction (from published rotor inertias) puts a QDD's airgap at ~13 kPa, which is the textbook figure for an air-cooled BLDC."
  },
